-- X16 (fix-round F1, §6.5) the `putStrLn` twin of x12/x14 -- and the row that
-- records WHY `putStrLn` is not, and cannot be, fixed by pinning the auto-print
-- wrap.
--
-- The wrap half IS fixed: slice 1's wrap synthesized a bare `EVar "putStrLn"`
-- into the ENTRY module, and `markVar` unions PROGRAM interface-method names
-- into the set it consults, so the interface below captured the wrap's outer
-- call. No `putStrLn` reference is synthesized in user scope any more -- the
-- wrap calls the prelude's own `println` declaration under an unspellable
-- re-binding (`autoPrintPinCore`, compiler/driver/main_autoprint.mdk).
--
-- 🚨 WHAT THIS ROW NOW PINS IS A DIFFERENT, PRE-EXISTING DEFECT that the wrap
-- fix does not reach and was never in front of it: `markVar`'s `methods` set is
-- PROGRAM-GLOBAL, so a user interface method named `putStrLn` also captures the
-- PRELUDE's OWN body -- `println x = putStrLn (display x)`, stdlib/core.mdk.
-- Unlike `display`, `putStrLn` is a plain runtime extern and the prelude
-- declares NO interface method of that name, so there is no prelude
-- declaration for the entry module's method table to prefer: the user's is the
-- only candidate, and the prelude's own call routes to it.
--
-- MEASURED, with the wrap taken out of the picture entirely -- a UNIT main and
-- a USER-WRITTEN `println` call, so `shouldAutoPrintMain` is False and nothing
-- is ever wrapped:
--     interface Ifc c where
--       putStrLn : c -> Int
--     impl Ifc Int where
--       putStrLn n = n + 1000
--     main = println 7
-- `medaka build` (MEDAKA_STRICT=1) exits 1 with
--   `error: emitter failed compiling …`
--   `runtime error [E-PANIC]: no impl of method 'putStrLn' for type 'Unit'`
-- -- byte-identical to what this fixture produces. The wrap is not involved.
--
-- So this row is graded ACCEPT|REJECT|REJECT|NONE: it pins the CURRENT state,
-- including the `check` ACCEPTs / `build` panics split, and it self-drains --
-- the day the prelude's own body stops being capturable, build starts ACCEPTing
-- and this row goes RED, which is the signal to re-grade it to
-- ACCEPT|REJECT|ACCEPT|BUILD_EXACT|7 (the value `main` actually holds).
-- Severity is S1, not S0: it is a loud emitter panic at exit 1, not a
-- right-looking wrong answer.
interface Ifc c where
  putStrLn : c -> Int

impl Ifc Int where
  putStrLn n = n + 1000

main = 7
